# Neural Network ML Assignment #3
Team: Ifrah Zainab (ixz220004) and Kunju Menon (sxm220267)

## Dataset
The project uses the **Drug Consumption (Quantified)** dataset from the UCI
Machine Learning Repository:

<https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/373/drug+consumption+quantified>

The dataset is fetched at runtime with `ucimlrepo`. The `ecstasy` target is converted to two classes: `CL2` through `CL6` are grouped as use within the last decade (class 1), and the remaining
labels as no use or last use more than a decade ago (class 0).

## Data split
The data is split into training, validation, and test sets using a stratified
70/15/15 split.

## Requirements and installation
Use Python 3.10 or newer, and install the dependencies:

```powershell
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Reproduce the experiment results
Run the experiment with:

```powershell
python main.py --run-experiments --epochs 100 --batch-size 32 --seed 42
```

For just one run of the current best validation-selected configuration, use:

```powershell
python main.py --activation relu --hidden 64 32 --optimizer momentum --learning-rate 0.001 --momentum 0.9 --epochs 100 --batch-size 32 --seed 42
```

To compare SGD and momentum using the same activation, architecture, learning
rate, data split, initialization seed, and mini-batch order, run:

```powershell
python main.py --compare-optimizers --activation sigmoid --hidden 64 --learning-rate 0.001 --momentum 0.9 --epochs 100 --batch-size 32 --seed 42
```

This saves per-epoch validation losses to
`results/optimizer_comparison.csv` and a comparison plot to
`results/optimizer_comparison.png`. The output reports each optimizer's
minimum validation loss and the epoch where it occurred; use the curves to
compare their progress, since the epoch of the minimum alone does not prove
which optimizer converged faster.

## Output files
Experiments and optimizer comparisons write files to `results/`. Review the
output metrics and graphs there.